    "paper_year_title": "JEE Advanced 2010 (Paper 1)",
    "paper_year_short": "2010 (Paper 1)",
    "held_on": "2010-04-30T09:00:00.000Z",
    "question_text": "A $0.1 \\mathrm{~kg}$ mass is suspended from a wire of negligible mass. The length of the wire is $1 \\mathrm{~m}$ and its cross-sectional area is $4.9 \\times 10^{-7} \\mathrm{~m}^2$. If the mass is pulled a little in the vertically downward direction and released, it performs simple harmonic motion of angular frequency $140 \\mathrm{rad} \\mathrm{s}^{-1}$. If the Young's modulus of the material of the wire is $n \\times 10^9 \\mathrm{Nm}^{-2}$, the value of $n$ is",
    "question_image": null,
    "options": [],
    "correct_answer": "4",
    "solution_text": "$\\omega=\\sqrt{\\frac{K}{m}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{Y A}{l m}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\left(n \\times 10^9\\right)\\left(4.9 \\times 10^{-7}\\right)}{1 \\times 0.1}}$\nPutting, $\\omega=140 \\operatorname{rad} \\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ in above equation we get,\n$$\nn=4\n$$\n$\\therefore$ Answer is 4 .",
    "solution_image": null,
    "topic": "Young's Modulus and Breaking Stres"
  }
